# About The Hackathon

What started as “let’s just participate for fun” quickly turned into 24 hours of nonstop coding, wiring, debugging, caffeine, sensor failures, last-minute fixes, and somehow… a fully working prototype by the end of it.

CropSense was built during a 24-hour hackathon where our team wanted to create something practical, impactful, and genuinely useful beyond just being another software-only project. Since agriculture continues to play such a major role in everyday life, we decided to focus on smart farming and crop health monitoring.

The idea behind CropSense was simple:
Can we build a low-cost system capable of monitoring environmental conditions and detecting plant stress in real time?

To make that happen, we combined:
- ESP32 based IoT monitoring
- Environmental sensing
- NIR-based leaf health detection
- Real-time dashboard monitoring
- Telegram alert integration
- OLED live status display

One of the biggest challenges was integrating both hardware and software within such a limited timeframe while ensuring the system remained stable enough for a live demo.

Despite the sleep deprivation and debugging chaos at 3 AM, seeing the final system come alive — sensors responding, OLED updating, alerts triggering, and the dashboard working in real time — made the entire experience worth it.

More than just a project, this hackathon became an incredible hands-on learning experience in:
- Embedded systems
- Sensor interfacing
- IoT architecture
- Rapid prototyping
- Team collaboration
- Real-time debugging under pressure

And honestly, that’s what made the event memorable.
